Q1. What is Huffman Encoding?
Huffman Encoding is a lossless data compression technique that assigns variable-length binary codes to characters based on their frequency of occurrence.
Frequently occurring characters get shorter codes.
Less frequently occurring characters get longer codes.
It reduces the total number of bits required to store data.

Q2. Which tree is used in Huffman encoding? Give one Example.
Huffman Encoding uses a binary tree called a Huffman Tree.
It is a full binary tree in which:
Each leaf node represents a character and its frequency.
Each internal node represents the sum of the frequencies of its child nodes.
The left edge is assigned 0 and the right edge is assigned 1.
Example:
Consider three characters with frequencies: Character Frequency, A 5, B 7, C 10
First, combine A and B (5 + 7 = 12). Then combine the resulting node with C (12 + 10 = 22).
